For GNW Production Closure, the safest production profile is to use threshold signatures for live governance approvals, keep Shamir sharing only for escrow or recovery workflows, require every participant to sign the exact same domain-separated release payload, bind verified TEE measurement into that payload, and fail closed on every mismatch in threshold math, serialization, attestation, or signature handling.
Where you need a portable evidence artifact, package the approval set with deterministic CBOR and COSE so independent verifiers reconstruct identical bytes before signature verification.

## 1. Shamir vs TSS/BLS

For release governance, my recommendation is: use Shamir Secret Sharing only for offline custody, emergency recovery, or seed material distribution, and use threshold signatures for the actual release authorization path. NIST treats threshold schemes as the distributed-trust primitive for signatures and related cryptographic operations, and RFC 9591 gives you an interoperable two-round threshold Schnorr profile for production signing.

FROST is the best default when the signer set is a fixed governance committee and you want ordinary Schnorr-style signatures without reconstructing a full signing key during each release. RFC 9591 specifies FROST as a two-round threshold Schnorr protocol and explicitly supports validation of signature shares before final aggregation.

BLS is the right alternative when artifact size, signature aggregation, or downstream verification fan-out matters more than implementation simplicity. The current BLS work describes aggregation properties, and RFC 9380 standardizes the hash-to-curve mechanisms commonly used with BLS12-381 suites based on SHA-256.

**Production rule:** never reconstruct the governance signing secret in application memory for the online release path; if Shamir exists at all, keep it in a separate recovery plane with human ceremony, dual control, and no code path shared with the release service.

```ts
export type GovernanceMode =
  | 'shamir-escrow-only'
  | 'frost-ed25519'
  | 'bls12-381-aggregate';

export interface GovernanceProfile {
  mode: GovernanceMode;
  onlineSigningAllowed: boolean;
  requiresShareReconstruction: boolean;
  signatureArtifact: 'detached-multi-sig' | 'single-threshold-signature' | 'aggregate-signature';
}

export const GOVERNANCE_PROFILES: Record<GovernanceMode, GovernanceProfile> = {
  'shamir-escrow-only': {
    mode: 'shamir-escrow-only',
    onlineSigningAllowed: false,
    requiresShareReconstruction: true,
    signatureArtifact: 'detached-multi-sig'
  },
  'frost-ed25519': {
    mode: 'frost-ed25519',
    onlineSigningAllowed: true,
    requiresShareReconstruction: false,
    signatureArtifact: 'single-threshold-signature'
  },
  'bls12-381-aggregate': {
    mode: 'bls12-381-aggregate',
    onlineSigningAllowed: true,
    requiresShareReconstruction: false,
    signatureArtifact: 'aggregate-signature'
  }
};

export function assertValidGovernanceMode(mode: GovernanceMode): void {
  const p = GOVERNANCE_PROFILES[mode];
  if (!p) throw new Error('ERR_UNSUPPORTED_GOVERNANCE_MODE');
  if (mode === 'shamir-escrow-only' && p.onlineSigningAllowed) {
    throw new Error('ERR_SHAMIR_MISCONFIGURED');
  }
}
```

## 2. Dynamic k-of-n threshold math

The minimum mathematical validity constraint is simple: $$1 \le k \le n$$ and $$n \ge 1$$. That is enough for a threshold authorization primitive, but it is not enough for robust governance because it does not guarantee quorum intersection, split-brain resistance, or Byzantine safety. 

For governance that must prevent two disjoint approvals from authorizing conflicting releases, require majority intersection with $$k > n/2$$. For Byzantine committee reasoning, a standard engineering rule is to size the committee as $$n \ge 3f + 1$$ and require $$k \ge 2f + 1$$, which ensures any two valid quorums intersect in at least $$f + 1$$ members and therefore cannot both be fully adversarial.

In practice, define threshold policy by mode, not by a raw integer alone. A human governance committee usually wants one of three policies: simple threshold auth, majority quorum, or BFT quorum certificate.

```ts
export type ThresholdMode = 'threshold-auth' | 'majority' | 'bft';

export interface ThresholdPolicy {
  n: number;
  k: number;
  mode: ThresholdMode;
}

export function validateThresholdPolicy(p: ThresholdPolicy): void {
  if (!Number.isInteger(p.n) || !Number.isInteger(p.k)) {
    throw new Error('ERR_THRESHOLD_NOT_INTEGER');
  }
  if (p.n < 1) throw new Error('ERR_N_LT_1');
  if (p.k < 1) throw new Error('ERR_K_LT_1');
  if (p.k > p.n) throw new Error('ERR_K_GT_N');

  if (p.mode === 'majority' && p.k <= Math.floor(p.n / 2)) {
    throw new Error('ERR_NO_QUORUM_INTERSECTION');
  }

  if (p.mode === 'bft') {
    const f = Math.floor((p.n - 1) / 3);
    if (p.n < 3 * f + 1) throw new Error('ERR_INVALID_BFT_N');
    if (p.k < 2 * f + 1) throw new Error('ERR_K_BELOW_BFT_QUORUM');
  }
}

export function computeRecommendedThreshold(n: number, mode: ThresholdMode): number {
  if (!Number.isInteger(n) || n < 1) throw new Error('ERR_BAD_N');

  switch (mode) {
    case 'threshold-auth':
      return Math.max(1, Math.ceil((2 * n) / 3));
    case 'majority':
      return Math.floor(n / 2) + 1;
    case 'bft': {
      const f = Math.floor((n - 1) / 3);
      return 2 * f + 1;
    }
  }
}
```

**Fail-closed invariant:** the release service must reject a policy update if changing $$n$$ or $$k$$ would invalidate any existing mode constraint, and it must reject any approval bundle evaluated under a different policy version than the one recorded at release creation time.

## 3. Sybil defense and distinct participant keys

A unique public key is not the same thing as a unique participant, so the participants map must bind three identities together: governance identity, cryptographic key identity, and hardware or attestation identity. NIST’s threshold cryptography project explicitly includes auxiliary techniques such as zero-knowledge proofs because participant correctness and identity binding are part of secure threshold deployments, not optional extras.

At enrollment, require proof of possession of the private key, require uniqueness of the canonical public-key fingerprint, require uniqueness of the governance subject identifier, and require an attested device or HSM identity that is not already bound elsewhere. RFC 8235 is useful here because Schnorr NIZK is explicitly described as a building block that proves knowledge of a discrete logarithm and helps ensure participants follow protocol honestly.

For BLS-based systems, distinct key verification also protects against rogue-key style enrollment failures, so a possession proof or signed challenge is mandatory before a key enters the active participants map. The defense is not just “the bytes differ”; it is “the bytes differ, the claimant controls the secret, the governance identity is unique, and the attested hardware root is unique.”

```ts
import { createHash, verify as nodeVerify } from 'node:crypto';

export interface EnrollmentRecord {
  participantId: string;
  governanceSubjectId: string;
  publicKeyDer: Buffer;
  proofOfPossessionSig: Buffer;
  attestedHardwareId: string;
  algorithm: 'ed25519' | 'frost-ed25519' | 'bls12-381';
}

export function sha256(buf: Buffer): Buffer {
  return createHash('sha256').update(buf).digest();
}

export function publicKeyFingerprint(publicKeyDer: Buffer): string {
  return sha256(publicKeyDer).toString('hex');
}

export function enrollmentChallenge(participantId: string, governanceSubjectId: string, hwId: string): Buffer {
  return Buffer.from(
    `GNW-ENROLL-V1|${participantId}|${governanceSubjectId}|${hwId}`,
    'utf8'
  );
}

export function verifyEd25519Possession(publicKeyPem: string, challenge: Buffer, sig: Buffer): boolean {
  return nodeVerify(null, challenge, publicKeyPem, sig);
}

export function assertDistinctEnrollment(
  rec: EnrollmentRecord,
  existingFingerprints: Set<string>,
  existingSubjects: Set<string>,
  existingHardwareIds: Set<string>
): void {
  const fp = publicKeyFingerprint(rec.publicKeyDer);

  if (existingFingerprints.has(fp)) throw new Error('ERR_DUPLICATE_PUBLIC_KEY');
  if (existingSubjects.has(rec.governanceSubjectId)) throw new Error('ERR_DUPLICATE_GOVERNANCE_SUBJECT');
  if (existingHardwareIds.has(rec.attestedHardwareId)) throw new Error('ERR_DUPLICATE_HARDWARE_ROOT');
}
```

**Fail-closed invariant:** no participant becomes active until all four checks pass: unique participant ID, unique governance subject, unique public-key fingerprint, and successful proof of possession.

## 4. Domain-separated release digest

The release digest should be exactly the user-specified construction `SHA256('GNW-RELEASE-V1|' + releaseSubject)`, encoded as UTF-8 with no whitespace trimming, no Unicode normalization, and no alternate separators. FIPS 180-4 defines SHA-256 as an approved secure hash algorithm producing a 256-bit digest, and NIST’s hash guidance positions SHA-256 as an approved digest algorithm for message integrity checking. 

Domain separation is not cosmetic here; it prevents cross-protocol signing ambiguity. A release subject signed in one GNW context must not verify as valid input for enrollment, rotation, attestation, or some future governance verb.

```ts
import { createHash } from 'node:crypto';

const RELEASE_PREFIX = 'GNW-RELEASE-V1|';

export function sha256HexUtf8(input: string): string {
  return createHash('sha256').update(Buffer.from(input, 'utf8')).digest('hex');
}

export function computeReleaseDigest(releaseSubject: string): string {
  if (typeof releaseSubject !== 'string' || releaseSubject.length === 0) {
    throw new Error('ERR_EMPTY_RELEASE_SUBJECT');
  }
  if (releaseSubject.includes('\u0000')) throw new Error('ERR_NUL_IN_SUBJECT');
  return sha256HexUtf8(RELEASE_PREFIX + releaseSubject);
}
```

**Fail-closed assertions:** reject empty `releaseSubject`, reject embedded NUL, reject any request that presents a precomputed digest without the original `releaseSubject`, and recompute the digest server-side every time.

## 5. Multi-party payload format

The exact payload to be signed should be the ASCII string `GNW-TRUST-ANCHOR-V1|{releaseDigest}|{measurement}|{nonce}`. That gives you domain separation at the payload layer and binds the release decision to both the approved subject digest and the TEE measurement. 

If you need a transport artifact, wrap that payload as the application content carried in a deterministic CBOR structure and sign it with COSE. RFC 8949 requires deterministic encoding rules such as preferred serialization and sorted map keys, and RFC 9052 defines COSE signing structures and the `Sig_structure` used to derive the exact bytes to sign.

COSE is especially useful when you want multiple signatures or an auditable envelope because `COSE_Sign` is explicitly defined for one or more signatures over the same content.

```ts
import { randomBytes } from 'node:crypto';

const PAYLOAD_PREFIX = 'GNW-TRUST-ANCHOR-V1|';

export interface ReleasePayload {
  releaseDigest: string;   // 64 hex chars
  measurement: string;     // 64+ hex chars, policy-defined
  nonce: string;           // 32 hex chars minimum
}

function assertLowerHex(value: string, minBytes: number, name: string): void {
  if (!/^[0-9a-f]+$/.test(value)) throw new Error(`ERR_${name}_NOT_HEX`);
  if (value.length < minBytes * 2) throw new Error(`ERR_${name}_TOO_SHORT`);
  if (value.length % 2 !== 0) throw new Error(`ERR_${name}_ODD_HEX_LENGTH`);
}

export function generateNonceHex(bytes = 16): string {
  if (bytes < 16) throw new Error('ERR_NONCE_BYTES_TOO_SMALL');
  return randomBytes(bytes).toString('hex');
}

export function buildMpcPayload(p: ReleasePayload): Buffer {
  assertLowerHex(p.releaseDigest, 32, 'RELEASE_DIGEST');
  assertLowerHex(p.measurement, 32, 'MEASUREMENT');
  assertLowerHex(p.nonce, 16, 'NONCE');

  return Buffer.from(
    `${PAYLOAD_PREFIX}${p.releaseDigest}|${p.measurement}|${p.nonce}`,
    'utf8'
  );
}
```

**Fail-closed invariant:** all participants must sign byte-for-byte identical payload bytes; never let each client “reconstruct” the string from local fields after partial normalization or formatting.

## 6. Duplicate signature deduplication

Deduplication must happen at two layers: in-memory during verification and in the database during persistence. The authoritative dedup key is `participantId`, not raw signature bytes, because the same participant may submit the same signature twice or submit different malformed artifacts for the same release.

The cryptographic set is therefore a set of verified participant identities attached to one release. A duplicate from an already-counted participant is ignored or rejected, but it must never increase the quorum count.

```ts
export interface SignatureSubmission {
  participantId: string;
  keyVersion: number;
  signature: Buffer;
}

export function dedupeByParticipantId(
  submissions: SignatureSubmission[]
): SignatureSubmission[] {
  const seen = new Set<string>();
  const deduped: SignatureSubmission[] = [];

  for (const s of submissions) {
    if (!s.participantId) throw new Error('ERR_MISSING_PARTICIPANT_ID');
    if (seen.has(s.participantId)) continue;
    seen.add(s.participantId);
    deduped.push(s);
  }
  return deduped;
}

export function assertQuorumUniqueParticipantCount(
  verifiedParticipantIds: Set<string>,
  k: number
): void {
  if (verifiedParticipantIds.size < k) throw new Error('ERR_QUORUM_NOT_MET');
}
```

At the SQL layer, enforce `unique(release_id, participant_id)` so application bugs cannot double-count the same signer. The release result should be computed from the cardinality of the verified unique participant set only.

## 7. Enforcing `requireTee=true` and binding measurement to consensus

If `requireTee=true`, treat remote attestation as a hard authorization dependency, not as metadata. RFC 9334’s RATS architecture is useful here because it cleanly separates Attester, Verifier, and Relying Party roles, and it frames Evidence and Attestation Results as inputs that must be appraised against policy before a relying decision is made.

The correct binding rule is: the measurement claim validated by the Verifier must exactly match the `measurement` field in the signed MPC payload, and that measurement must be allowed by the release policy for the target environment. If these values are not identical, the release is invalid even if threshold signatures are otherwise correct.

```ts
export interface TeeEvidence {
  verifierId: string;
  attesterId: string;
  measurementSha256: string;
  appraisalResult: 'trusted' | 'untrusted';
  issuedAt: string;
  expiresAt: string;
  evidenceDigest: string;
}

export interface ReleasePolicy {
  requireTee: boolean;
  allowedMeasurements: Set<string>;
}

export function assertTeeBound(
  policy: ReleasePolicy,
  payloadMeasurement: string,
  evidence: TeeEvidence | null,
  now = new Date()
): void {
  if (!policy.requireTee) return;
  if (!evidence) throw new Error('ERR_TEE_EVIDENCE_REQUIRED');
  if (evidence.appraisalResult !== 'trusted') throw new Error('ERR_TEE_NOT_TRUSTED');
  if (evidence.measurementSha256 !== payloadMeasurement) {
    throw new Error('ERR_MEASUREMENT_NOT_BOUND');
  }
  if (!policy.allowedMeasurements.has(payloadMeasurement)) {
    throw new Error('ERR_MEASUREMENT_NOT_ALLOWED');
  }
  if (Date.parse(evidence.expiresAt) <= now.getTime()) {
    throw new Error('ERR_TEE_EVIDENCE_EXPIRED');
  }
}
```

**Fail-closed assertions:** no TEE result means no release, an expired TEE result means no release, an untrusted appraisal means no release, and a measurement mismatch between attestation and signed payload means no release. The signer set is authorizing a specific measured runtime, not merely a release label.

## 8. Byzantine handling without crashing

A malicious participant must be treated as invalid input, not as a process-fatal event. RFC 9591 is helpful operationally because FROST defines share validation and the underlying FROST work explicitly discusses aborting on misbehavior while identifying and excluding bad participants from future operations.

Your verifier should therefore process all submissions independently, classify each one as valid, duplicate, stale, unauthorized, or cryptographically invalid, and continue collecting until either $$k$$ valid unique participants are reached or no remaining path can satisfy quorum. One bad signature must never crash the release worker, poison the batch, or increment the approval count.

```ts
export type VerifyOutcome =
  | 'valid'
  | 'duplicate'
  | 'unknown-participant'
  | 'inactive-key'
  | 'bad-signature'
  | 'policy-mismatch'
  | 'tee-mismatch';

export interface VerifiedApproval {
  participantId: string;
  keyVersion: number;
  outcome: VerifyOutcome;
}

export function evaluateApprovals(
  submissions: SignatureSubmission[],
  verifyOne: (s: SignatureSubmission) => VerifyOutcome,
  k: number
): { approvals: VerifiedApproval[]; quorumMet: boolean } {
  const seen = new Set<string>();
  const approvals: VerifiedApproval[] = [];
  let valid = 0;

  for (const s of submissions) {
    if (seen.has(s.participantId)) {
      approvals.push({ participantId: s.participantId, keyVersion: s.keyVersion, outcome: 'duplicate' });
      continue;
    }
    seen.add(s.participantId);

    const outcome = verifyOne(s);
    approvals.push({ participantId: s.participantId, keyVersion: s.keyVersion, outcome });

    if (outcome === 'valid') valid++;
    if (valid >= k) return { approvals, quorumMet: true };
  }

  return { approvals, quorumMet: false };
}
```

**Fail-closed invariant:** the only successful terminal state is `quorumMet=true` with `k` unique valid participants under the same release policy, payload bytes, and measurement binding; every other state is a reject, never a warn-and-proceed.

## 9. Key rotation with historical audit integrity

Key rotation must create a new active verification key without invalidating old release records. NIST SP 800-57 is the controlling guidance here: private signature keys have a cryptoperiod and should be destroyed at the end of that cryptoperiod, while public verification keys may remain available for verification far longer because historical signatures must stay auditable.

That means you should version participant keys, store the key version with every approval record, preserve old public keys for verification, and forbid retroactive reassignment of a historical approval to a newer key. Rotation updates the active key set for new releases only.

```sql
create table mpc_participants (
  participant_id text primary key,
  governance_subject_id text not null unique,
  status text not null check (status in ('active','suspended','retired')),
  tee_required boolean not null default true,
  created_at timestamptz not null default now()
);

create table mpc_participant_keys (
  participant_id text not null references mpc_participants(participant_id),
  key_version integer not null,
  algorithm text not null check (algorithm in ('ed25519','frost-ed25519','bls12-381')),
  public_key_der bytea not null,
  public_key_sha256 bytea not null,
  proof_of_possession bytea not null,
  attested_hardware_id text not null,
  valid_from timestamptz not null,
  valid_to timestamptz,
  status text not null check (status in ('active','retired','revoked')),
  primary key (participant_id, key_version),
  unique (public_key_sha256),
  unique (attested_hardware_id)
);

create table mpc_key_rotations (
  participant_id text not null,
  old_key_version integer not null,
  new_key_version integer not null,
  rotated_at timestamptz not null default now(),
  approved_by_release_id uuid,
  primary key (participant_id, old_key_version, new_key_version),
  foreign key (participant_id, old_key_version)
    references mpc_participant_keys(participant_id, key_version),
  foreign key (participant_id, new_key_version)
    references mpc_participant_keys(participant_id, key_version)
);

create table mpc_releases (
  release_id uuid primary key,
  policy_version integer not null,
  threshold_n integer not null check (threshold_n >= 1),
  threshold_k integer not null check (threshold_k >= 1 and threshold_k <= threshold_n),
  release_subject text not null,
  release_digest char(64) not null unique,
  measurement_sha256 char(64) not null,
  nonce_hex char(32) not null,
  require_tee boolean not null,
  status text not null check (status in ('pending','approved','rejected')),
  created_at timestamptz not null default now()
);

create table mpc_release_approvals (
  release_id uuid not null references mpc_releases(release_id),
  participant_id text not null references mpc_participants(participant_id),
  key_version integer not null,
  signature bytea not null,
  outcome text not null check (outcome in (
    'valid','duplicate','unknown-participant','inactive-key',
    'bad-signature','policy-mismatch','tee-mismatch'
  )),
  created_at timestamptz not null default now(),
  primary key (release_id, participant_id),
  foreign key (participant_id, key_version)
    references mpc_participant_keys(participant_id, key_version)
);
```

**Rotation protocol:** enroll new key with proof of possession and attestation, mark old key `retired` with `valid_to`, activate new key with `valid_from`, and store an immutable rotation edge in `mpc_key_rotations`. Historical approvals continue verifying against the recorded historical public key.

## 10. Fail-closed preflight test harness

The preflight harness should be a deterministic verifier that runs before any release can move to `approved`. It should rebuild the release digest, rebuild the exact payload bytes, validate TEE policy, validate participant uniqueness, validate key status and version, verify every signature, and assert that the final accepted signer set satisfies the policy without duplicates or stale data.

If you serialize evidence as CBOR or COSE, the harness must also reject malformed or non-deterministic encodings. RFC 8949 says decoders must not return decoded data for malformed CBOR and defines deterministic encoding requirements, while RFC 9052 defines the canonical `Sig_structure` that must be reconstructed identically before signature verification.

```ts
import test from 'node:test';
import assert from 'node:assert/strict';

export interface PreflightInput {
  policy: ThresholdPolicy & ReleasePolicy;
  releaseSubject: string;
  payload: ReleasePayload;
  evidence: TeeEvidence | null;
  submissions: SignatureSubmission[];
}

export function preflightValidate(input: PreflightInput): void {
  validateThresholdPolicy(input.policy);

  const recomputedDigest = computeReleaseDigest(input.releaseSubject);
  if (recomputedDigest !== input.payload.releaseDigest) {
    throw new Error('ERR_RELEASE_DIGEST_MISMATCH');
  }

  const payloadBytes = buildMpcPayload(input.payload);
  if (payloadBytes.length < 64) throw new Error('ERR_PAYLOAD_TOO_SHORT');

  assertTeeBound(input.policy, input.payload.measurement, input.evidence);

  const deduped = dedupeByParticipantId(input.submissions);
  if (deduped.length < input.policy.k) throw new Error('ERR_INSUFFICIENT_UNIQUE_SUBMISSIONS');

  // Plug in real cryptographic verification here.
  const verifiedIds = new Set<string>();
  for (const s of deduped) {
    const ok = s.signature.length > 0; // replace with actual verifier
    if (!ok) throw new Error(`ERR_BAD_SIGNATURE:${s.participantId}`);
    verifiedIds.add(s.participantId);
  }

  assertQuorumUniqueParticipantCount(verifiedIds, input.policy.k);
}

test('rejects duplicate signer inflation', () => {
  const policy: ThresholdPolicy & ReleasePolicy = {
    n: 5, k: 3, mode: 'majority',
    requireTee: true,
    allowedMeasurements: new Set(['a'.repeat(64)])
  };

  const payload: ReleasePayload = {
    releaseDigest: computeReleaseDigest('release:prod:2026-10-21'),
    measurement: 'a'.repeat(64),
    nonce: 'b'.repeat(32)
  };

  assert.throws(() => preflightValidate({
    policy,
    releaseSubject: 'release:prod:2026-10-21',
    payload,
    evidence: {
      verifierId: 'verifier-1',
      attesterId: 'tee-1',
      measurementSha256: 'a'.repeat(64),
      appraisalResult: 'trusted',
      issuedAt: new Date().toISOString(),
      expiresAt: new Date(Date.now() + 60_000).toISOString(),
      evidenceDigest: 'c'.repeat(64)
    },
    submissions: [
      { participantId: 'p1', keyVersion: 1, signature: Buffer.from('01', 'hex') },
      { participantId: 'p1', keyVersion: 1, signature: Buffer.from('02', 'hex') },
      { participantId: 'p2', keyVersion: 1, signature: Buffer.from('03', 'hex') }
    ]
  }), /ERR_INSUFFICIENT_UNIQUE_SUBMISSIONS/);
});
```

The harness should include negative tests for at least these cases: bad threshold math, duplicate participant IDs, duplicate public keys, stale or missing TEE evidence, measurement mismatch, wrong release digest, wrong nonce length, inactive key version, malformed signature, mixed policy versions, and non-deterministic serialization if COSE or CBOR artifacts are used.
Operationally, make preflight a mandatory gate in CI and in the production approval service, and make it impossible for an operator override to bypass a failed cryptographic invariant without creating a separate emergency record outside the normal release path.

