# Release and Git Provenance Binding for GNW Production Closure

**Terminology correction.** SLSA v1.0 no longer defines a "Level 4". The former L4 requirements, hermetic and reproducible builds, were deferred rather than mapped one-to-one, so "SLSA 4" no longer names a current requirement. The spec now defines Build L1 to L3. L3 requires a hardened platform that isolates runs from each other and keeps provenance-signing secrets away from user-defined build steps. This design treats **Build L3 as the normative floor**. It then adds reproducibility (section 7), a hermetic build and an allowlist as GNW-specific "L4-equivalent" controls. Do not describe the result as "SLSA L4" in audits.

All code below is TypeScript on Node 20+, with `execFile` and no shell. Every check throws, so the pipeline fails closed.

## 1. Commit SHA format validation (5.1)

Git object names are either 40-hex (SHA-1) or 64-hex (SHA-256). Repositories created with `git init --object-format=sha256` use 64-hex IDs, and existing SHA-1 repositories cannot be converted in place. Git also has a translation-table design so that both formats can be used during a transition.

The invariants are:
- The validator must accept only the format the repository actually uses. Reading `git rev-parse --show-object-format` removes the ambiguity.
- It must reject uppercase hex, short SHAs, ref names and whitespace. Otherwise an attacker can smuggle in `HEAD`, `main` or `abc123`, which are ambiguous.
- The SHA must resolve to a commit object, not a tag or tree.

```ts
// git-sha.ts
import { execFile } from "node:child_process";
import { promisify } from "node:util";
const run = promisify(execFile);

export const SHA1_RE = /^[0-9a-f]{40}$/;
export const SHA256_RE = /^[0-9a-f]{64}$/;
export type ObjectFormat = "sha1" | "sha256";

export async function git(args: string[], cwd = process.cwd()): Promise<string> {
  const { stdout } = await run("git", args, { cwd, maxBuffer: 64 << 20,
    env: { ...process.env, GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT: "0", LC_ALL: "C" } });
  return stdout.trim();
}

export async function repoFormat(cwd?: string): Promise<ObjectFormat> {
  const f = await git(["rev-parse", "--show-object-format"], cwd);
  if (f !== "sha1" && f !== "sha256") throw new Error(`unknown object format: ${f}`);
  return f;
}

export async function assertCommitSha(raw: string, cwd?: string): Promise<string> {
  const fmt = await repoFormat(cwd);
  const re = fmt === "sha1" ? SHA1_RE : SHA256_RE;
  if (typeof raw !== "string" || !re.test(raw))
    throw new Error(`INVARIANT: commit SHA must be full lowercase ${fmt === "sha1" ? 40 : 64}-hex`);
  const type = await git(["cat-file", "-t", raw], cwd).catch(() => "");
  if (type !== "commit") throw new Error(`INVARIANT: ${raw} is not a commit object (got '${type}')`);
  return raw;
}
```

## 2. Environment precedence (5.2)

The rule is **explicit override, then CI default, then verified against `git rev-parse HEAD`**. `GNW_RELEASE_COMMIT_SHA` is an operator-controlled value, so it takes precedence. It is only a *claim*, however, and must equal the checked-out HEAD. If both variables are set and differ, the build aborts.

On `pull_request` events, `GITHUB_SHA` is the synthetic merge commit, not the PR head. Release workflows should therefore run only on `push` to protected refs or on tag events. The code enforces this indirectly: the resolved SHA must equal HEAD, and HEAD must be an ancestor of the protected branch or the tag target.

```ts
// resolve-commit.ts
import { assertCommitSha, git } from "./git-sha";

export async function resolveReleaseCommit(env = process.env, cwd?: string) {
  const override = env.GNW_RELEASE_COMMIT_SHA?.trim();
  const ci = env.GITHUB_SHA?.trim();
  if (override && ci && override !== ci)
    throw new Error(`INVARIANT: GNW_RELEASE_COMMIT_SHA (${override}) != GITHUB_SHA (${ci})`);
  const claimed = override ?? ci;
  if (!claimed) throw new Error("INVARIANT: no commit SHA in environment");
  await assertCommitSha(claimed, cwd);
  const head = await git(["rev-parse", "HEAD^{commit}"], cwd);
  if (head !== claimed) throw new Error(`INVARIANT: HEAD ${head} != claimed ${claimed}`);
  return { sha: claimed, source: override ? "GNW_RELEASE_COMMIT_SHA" : "GITHUB_SHA" } as const;
}
```

## 3. Clean working tree (5.3)

`git status --porcelain=v2 -z --untracked-files=all --ignore-submodules=none` is machine-stable and covers modified, staged and untracked entries. Submodules need a separate check. A submodule prefixed `+` in `git submodule status --recursive` is at a different commit than the one recorded in the superproject. A `-` prefix means it is uninitialised, and `U` means it has merge conflicts. Ignored files are excluded from the status output on purpose, but a release build should still run on a fresh clone so they cannot affect it.

```ts
// clean-tree.ts
import { git } from "./git-sha";

export async function assertCleanTree(cwd?: string) {
  const st = await git(["status","--porcelain=v2","-z","--untracked-files=all","--ignore-submodules=none"], cwd);
  if (st.length) throw new Error(`INVARIANT: dirty tree:\n${st.split("\0").slice(0,20).join("\n")}`);
  const subs = await git(["submodule","status","--recursive"], cwd);
  for (const l of subs.split("\n").filter(Boolean))
    if (!/^ [0-9a-f]{40,64} /.test(l)) throw new Error(`INVARIANT: submodule not pristine: ${l}`);
  const shallow = await git(["rev-parse","--is-shallow-repository"], cwd);
  if (shallow === "true") throw new Error("INVARIANT: shallow clone; ancestry checks unreliable");
}
```

## 4. SLSA provenance binding (5.4)

Provenance must bind three things: the source commit, the build workflow identity, and the artifact digest. The SLSA v1 provenance predicate (`https://slsa.dev/provenance/v1`) does this inside an in-toto Statement v1. The `subject` carries the artifact digest and `buildDefinition.externalParameters` and `resolvedDependencies` carry the source commit. `runDetails.builder.id` carries the workflow identity.

Build L3 requires the signing material to be unreachable from user build steps. In practice that means generating provenance in a **separate reusable workflow**, such as `slsa-framework/slsa-github-generator`, rather than in the build job. Alternatively, `cosign attest-blob` wraps an in-toto statement in a DSSE envelope and signs it keylessly.

```ts
// provenance.ts
export interface Statement {
  _type: "https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1";
  subject: { name: string; digest: { sha256: string } }[];
  predicateType: "https://slsa.dev/provenance/v1";
  predicate: unknown;
}
export function buildStatement(a: { name: string; sha256: string }, commit: string, env = process.env): Statement {
  if (!/^[0-9a-f]{64}$/.test(a.sha256)) throw new Error("bad artifact digest");
  const repo = `https://github.com/${env.GITHUB_REPOSITORY}`;
  return {
    _type: "https://in-toto.io/Statement/v1",
    subject: [{ name: a.name, digest: { sha256: a.sha256 } }],
    predicateType: "https://slsa.dev/provenance/v1",
    predicate: {
      buildDefinition: {
        buildType: "https://gnw.example/buildtypes/release/v1",
        externalParameters: { workflow: env.GITHUB_WORKFLOW_REF, ref: env.GITHUB_REF },
        resolvedDependencies: [{ uri: `git+${repo}@${env.GITHUB_REF}`,
          digest: { gitCommit: commit } }],
      },
      runDetails: {
        builder: { id: `${repo}/${env.GITHUB_WORKFLOW_REF}` },
        metadata: { invocationId: `${repo}/actions/runs/${env.GITHUB_RUN_ID}/attempts/${env.GITHUB_RUN_ATTEMPT}` },
      },
    },
  };
}
```

```yaml
# .github/workflows/release.yml (permissions minimal, signing job isolated)
permissions: { contents: read }
jobs:
  attest:
    needs: build
    permissions: { id-token: write, attestations: write }
    steps:
      - run: cosign attest-blob --yes --predicate prov.json --type slsaprovenance1 --bundle gnw.bundle artifact.tgz
```

Verification pins the signer identity, never "any valid signature" (see section 10).

## 5. Tag signing: GPG and Sigstore (5.5)

**GPG.** Use `git tag -s` and verify with `git verify-tag`. The invariant is stricter than "gpg exit 0". Parse `--raw` status output for `VALIDSIG <fpr>` and require that the primary-key fingerprint is in a pinned allowlist. This avoids trusting whatever keyring happens to be on the machine. Reject `EXPKEYSIG`, `REVKEYSIG` and `BADSIG`.

**Sigstore keyless.** Keyless signing authenticates through OIDC, gets a short-lived Fulcio certificate, and records the event in the Rekor transparency log. The identity comes from the OIDC token, not from a label passed to `cosign`. The Fulcio certificate lives about 10 minutes, so verification relies on trusted timestamp evidence from Rekor or an RFC 3161 authority. For Git tags, sign the tag object's bytes as a blob and verify with pinned identity and issuer.

```ts
// tag-verify.ts
import { execFile } from "node:child_process";
import { promisify } from "node:util";
const run = promisify(execFile);

export async function verifyGpgTag(tag: string, allowedFprs: Set<string>) {
  const r = await run("git", ["verify-tag", "--raw", tag]).catch(e => e);
  const out: string = (r.stderr ?? "") + (r.stdout ?? "");
  if (/\[GNUPG:\] (BADSIG|EXPKEYSIG|REVKEYSIG|ERRSIG)/.test(out)) throw new Error("INVARIANT: bad tag signature");
  const m = out.match(/\[GNUPG:\] VALIDSIG \S+ \S+ \S+ \S+ \S+ \S+ \S+ \S+ \S+ ([0-9A-F]{40})/);
  if (!m || !allowedFprs.has(m)) throw new Error("INVARIANT: tag signer not in allowlist");
  return m;
}

export async function verifyCosignTag(tagObjPath: string, sigPath: string, certPath: string) {
  await run("cosign", ["verify-blob", tagObjPath, "--signature", sigPath, "--certificate", certPath,
    "--certificate-identity", "https://github.com/ORG/REPO/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/heads/main",
    "--certificate-oidc-issuer", "https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com"]);
}
```

The tag object bytes come from `git cat-file tag <tag>`. Also require that `git rev-parse <tag>^{commit}` equals the resolved commit from section 2. Tags are mutable pointers, so this binding is what makes the tag trustworthy.

## 6. Deterministic artifact digests (5.6)

The artifact digest must not depend on the machine, so the archive itself has to be canonical. That means sorted entries, fixed mtime (`SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH`), uid and gid of 0, normalised modes, no PAX extended headers carrying host data, and gzip with no name or timestamp in the header. The digest is the SHA-256 (FIPS 180-4) of the final bytes. Publish it as `sha256:<hex>` and refer to OCI artifacts only by digest, never by tag.

```ts
// digest.ts
import { createHash } from "node:crypto";
import { createReadStream } from "node:fs";
import { pipeline } from "node:stream/promises";

export async function sha256File(p: string) {
  const h = createHash("sha256");
  await pipeline(createReadStream(p), h);
  return h.digest("hex");
}
// canonical tar (GNU tar), run inside the build container
export const TAR_CMD = (dir: string, out: string) => [
  "sh","-c",
  `tar --sort=name --mtime=@$SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH --owner=0 --group=0 --numeric-owner ` +
  `--format=gnu --pax-option=delete=atime,delete=ctime -cf - -C ${dir} . | gzip -n -9 > ${out}`];
```

Multi-machine agreement is checked in section 7. A distributed build is accepted only when at least two independent builders emit the same digest.

## 7. Reproducible build verification (5.7)

Pin the base image by digest, pin the toolchain and dependencies with lockfiles, and set `SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH` to the commit timestamp. Run `TZ=UTC`, `LC_ALL=C`, a fixed `umask 022`, and no network access after dependency fetch. Then build in N independent containers, ideally on different hosts, and compare the digests. A mismatch fails the release. Use `diffoscope` only for diagnosis, never as the pass criterion.

```ts
// repro.ts
import { sha256File } from "./digest";
export async function assertReproducible(paths: string[]) {
  if (paths.length < 2) throw new Error("INVARIANT: need >=2 independent builds");
  const d = await Promise.all(paths.map(sha256File));
  if (new Set(d).size !== 1) throw new Error(`INVARIANT: non-reproducible: ${d.join(" vs ")}`);
  return d;
}
```

```bash
export SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH=$(git log -1 --format=%ct "$SHA")
docker run --rm --network=none --read-only -e SOURCE_DATE_EPOCH -e TZ=UTC \
  builder@sha256:<PINNED> /build.sh
```

## 8. Canonical release metadata (5.8)

Use RFC 8785 (JSON Canonicalization Scheme, JCS) so the signed bytes are unambiguous. The schema is closed (`additionalProperties: false`) and carries the commit, artifact digests and builder identity. The signature is **detached or in a wrapper envelope**, never embedded inside the signed bytes. Embedding a signature in the same object it covers creates a circular definition. The standard solution is a DSSE envelope: `{payloadType, payload(base64), signatures[]}` with PAE (pre-authentication encoding).

```ts
// release-meta.ts
import Ajv from "ajv"; import canonicalize from "canonicalize"; // RFC 8785
export const schema = {
  $id: "https://gnw.example/schemas/release/v1.json", type: "object", additionalProperties: false,
  required: ["schemaVersion","commit","objectFormat","version","artifacts","builder","createdAt"],
  properties: {
    schemaVersion: { const: 1 },
    commit: { type: "string", pattern: "^([0-9a-f]{40}|[0-9a-f]{64})$" },
    objectFormat: { enum: ["sha1","sha256"] },
    version: { type: "string", pattern: "^\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+(-[0-9A-Za-z.-]+)?$" },
    artifacts: { type: "array", minItems: 1, items: { type: "object", additionalProperties: false,
      required: ["name","sha256"], properties: { name: { type: "string" },
      sha256: { type: "string", pattern: "^[0-9a-f]{64}$" } } } },
    builder: { type: "string" }, createdAt: { type: "string", format: "date-time" },
  },
};
const validate = new Ajv({ strict: true, allErrors: false }).compile(schema);
export function canonicalBytes(meta: unknown): Buffer {
  if (!validate(meta)) throw new Error("INVARIANT: schema: " + JSON.stringify(validate.errors));
  return Buffer.from(canonicalize(meta)!, "utf8");
}
export function dssePAE(type: string, body: Buffer) {
  return Buffer.concat([Buffer.from(`DSSEv1 ${type.length} ${type} ${body.length} `), body]);
}
```

Sign `dssePAE("application/vnd.gnw.release+json", canonicalBytes(meta))` with `cosign sign-blob` or `cosign attest-blob`. The verifier must also check that `meta.commit` matches the commit in the provenance.

## 9. Rollback allowlist (5.9)

A rollback target must satisfy three conditions:
- It is present in an **append-only, signed allowlist**.
- It exists as a commit object in the repository.
- It is an ancestor of the protected branch, so history cannot be rewritten to inject one.

The database enforces immutability with triggers. The allowlist itself is signed, and the signature is verified before the DB is trusted.

```sql
CREATE TABLE rollback_allowlist (
  id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  commit_sha TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (commit_sha ~ '^([0-9a-f]{40}|[0-9a-f]{64})$'),
  artifact_sha256 TEXT NOT NULL CHECK (artifact_sha256 ~ '^[0-9a-f]{64}$'),
  approved_by TEXT NOT NULL,
  attestation_bundle BYTEA NOT NULL,
  revoked_at TIMESTAMPTZ,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  UNIQUE (commit_sha, artifact_sha256)
);
CREATE FUNCTION deny_mutation() RETURNS trigger AS $$
BEGIN
  IF TG_OP = 'DELETE' THEN RAISE EXCEPTION 'allowlist is append-only'; END IF;
  IF NEW.commit_sha <> OLD.commit_sha OR NEW.artifact_sha256 <> OLD.artifact_sha256
     OR NEW.approved_by <> OLD.approved_by OR NEW.attestation_bundle <> OLD.attestation_bundle
     OR OLD.revoked_at IS NOT NULL THEN
    RAISE EXCEPTION 'only first-time revocation permitted'; END IF;
  RETURN NEW;
END $$ LANGUAGE plpgsql;
CREATE TRIGGER allowlist_immutable BEFORE UPDATE OR DELETE ON rollback_allowlist
  FOR EACH ROW EXECUTE FUNCTION deny_mutation();
```

```ts
// rollback.ts
import { Pool } from "pg"; import { assertCommitSha, git } from "./git-sha";
export async function validateRollback(pool: Pool, sha: string, artifactSha: string, protectedRef = "origin/main") {
  await assertCommitSha(sha);
  const { rowCount } = await pool.query(
    `SELECT 1 FROM rollback_allowlist WHERE commit_sha=$1 AND artifact_sha256=$2 AND revoked_at IS NULL`,
    [sha, artifactSha]);
  if (rowCount !== 1) throw new Error("INVARIANT: rollback target not in allowlist");
  await git(["merge-base", "--is-ancestor", sha, protectedRef]); // non-zero exit => throws
}
```

Never interpolate SHAs into SQL. Parameterised queries, plus the CHECK constraint, give defence in depth.

## 10. Pre-deployment audit script (5.10)

This script is the single gate before deploy, and every step must pass. It chains the earlier checks: commit resolution, clean tree, signed tag, then attestation verification with a pinned workflow identity. It finishes by recomputing the artifact digest and requiring a match against both the attestation subject and the release metadata. The pinned identity regexp follows the form shown in Sigstore verification guidance, and issuer pinning is mandatory.

```ts
// audit.ts
import { execFile } from "node:child_process";
import { promisify } from "node:util";
import { readFile } from "node:fs/promises";
import { resolveReleaseCommit } from "./resolve-commit";
import { assertCleanTree } from "./clean-tree";
import { verifyGpgTag } from "./tag-verify";
import { sha256File } from "./digest";
import { git } from "./git-sha";
const run = promisify(execFile);

export async function audit(o: { tag: string; artifact: string; bundle: string; allowedFprs: Set<string>;
  identity: string; issuer: string; metaPath: string }) {
  const { sha } = await resolveReleaseCommit();
  await assertCleanTree();
  const tagCommit = await git(["rev-parse", `${o.tag}^{commit}`]);
  if (tagCommit !== sha) throw new Error("INVARIANT: tag does not point at release commit");
  await verifyGpgTag(o.tag, o.allowedFprs);
  await git(["verify-commit", sha]);

  const digest = await sha256File(o.artifact);
  const { stdout } = await run("cosign", ["verify-blob-attestation", "--bundle", o.bundle,
    "--type", "slsaprovenance1", "--certificate-identity", o.identity,
    "--certificate-oidc-issuer", o.issuer, o.artifact]).catch(e => { throw new Error("INVARIANT: attestation verify failed: " + e.message); });

  const meta = JSON.parse(await readFile(o.metaPath, "utf8"));
  if (meta.commit !== sha) throw new Error("INVARIANT: metadata commit mismatch");
  if (!meta.artifacts.some((a: any) => a.sha256 === digest)) throw new Error("INVARIANT: artifact digest not in metadata");
  return { sha, digest, ok: true as const };
}
```

The exact `cosign` subcommand and flag names for blob attestation verification have changed across major versions. Pin the cosign version in CI and test this call against it. The audit should also write its result to an append-only log, and the deploy step must refuse to proceed on any thrown error.

## Fail-closed invariant summary

| Control | Assertion | Failure effect |
|---|---|---|
| Commit format | Lowercase 40/64-hex matching repo object format, type `commit` | Abort |
| Env precedence | Override and CI value must agree and equal HEAD | Abort |
| Tree state | Empty porcelain v2 output, pristine submodules, non-shallow | Abort |
| Provenance | Pinned signer identity and issuer, subject digest matches recomputed digest | Abort |
| Tags | Allowlisted GPG fingerprint or pinned Sigstore identity, points at release commit | Abort |
| Reproducibility | At least 2 independent builds produce identical SHA-256 | Abort |
| Metadata | Closed JSON schema, RFC 8785 canonical bytes, detached DSSE signature | Abort |
| Rollback | Allowlisted, not revoked, ancestor of protected branch | Abort |

P6.research:
Below is a production-grade SSRF and egress-control architecture that closes the major bypass classes you listed, with fail-closed semantics and Node.js/TypeScript implementation patterns. The core design is: validate the URL, resolve and pin the destination IP, enforce a strict allowlist, block all private/special-use networks, forbid unsafe redirects, and log every attempted egress with immutable evidence.

## 1. DNS rebinding defense

DNS rebinding is prevented by resolving the hostname once, validating every returned A and AAAA record, and then connecting only to the vetted IP address rather than letting the HTTP client re-resolve the hostname later. This avoids the classic TOCTOU gap where validation happens on one DNS answer and connection happens on another answer after the attacker changes the record.

Architecturally, the request pipeline should be: parse canonical URL → reject non-HTTPS → resolve DNS → classify each IP as allowed or blocked → choose a validated IP → connect to that literal IP while preserving the original hostname in SNI and `Host` for TLS and HTTP correctness. The invariant is: **no connection may occur unless at least one validated address exists and the exact address used for dialing is in the validated set**.

```ts
import dns from 'node:dns/promises';
import net from 'node:net';

function isPrivateOrSpecial(ip: string): boolean {
  const v = net.isIP(ip);
  if (!v) return true;
  if (v === 4) {
    const parts = ip.split('.').map(Number);
    const n = (parts << 24) | (parts << 16) | (parts << 8) | parts;
    const inRange = (base: number, mask: number) => (n & mask) === base;
    return (
      inRange(0x0a000000, 0xff000000) ||       // 10.0.0.0/8
      inRange(0xac100000, 0xfff00000) ||       // 172.16.0.0/12
      inRange(0xc0a80000, 0xffff0000) ||       // 192.168.0.0/16
      inRange(0x7f000000, 0xff000000) ||       // 127.0.0.0/8
      inRange(0xa9fe0000, 0xffff0000) ||       // 169.254.0.0/16
      inRange(0x64400000, 0xffc00000)          // 100.64.0.0/10
    );
  }
  const normalized = ip.toLowerCase();
  return (
    normalized === '::1' ||
    normalized.startsWith('fc') || normalized.startsWith('fd') || // fc00::/7
    normalized.startsWith('fe80:') ||                              // fe80::/10
    normalized === '::' ||
    normalized.startsWith('ff')                                    // multicast
  );
}

export async function resolveValidatedAddresses(hostname: string): Promise<string[]> {
  const [a, aaaa] = await Promise.all([
    dns.resolve4(hostname, { ttl: false }).catch(() => []),
    dns.resolve6(hostname, { ttl: false }).catch(() => []),
  ]);
  const ips = [...a, ...aaaa];
  const allowed = ips.filter(ip => !isPrivateOrSpecial(ip));
  if (allowed.length === 0) throw new Error('SSRF_BLOCKED_NO_VALID_PUBLIC_IP');
  return allowed;
}
```

## 2. Private IP blocking

Blocking must be expressed as an allowlist of globally routable destinations, not as a denylist of a few internal ranges. At minimum, reject RFC 1918 IPv4, loopback `127.0.0.0/8`, link-local `169.254.0.0/16`, IPv6 ULA `fc00::/7`, IPv6 link-local `fe80::/10`, loopback `::1`, and any multicast or unspecified address. In production, also consider CGNAT `100.64.0.0/10`, benchmarking ranges, documentation ranges, and IPv4-mapped IPv6 forms so attackers cannot bypass filters with alternate encodings.

The security invariant is simple: **every resolved address and every redirect target must independently pass address classification before dial**. Do not rely on string matching against hostnames, because DNS rebinding and alternate IP representations defeat it.

```ts
import { isIP } from 'node:net';

export function assertPublicAddress(ip: string): void {
  if (isPrivateOrSpecial(ip)) throw new Error(`SSRF_BLOCKED_PRIVATE_OR_SPECIAL_IP:${ip}`);
  if (!isIP(ip)) throw new Error(`INVALID_IP:${ip}`);
}
```

## 3. HTTPS and TLS 1.3

Enforce HTTPS-only outbound requests, reject `http:`, `ftp:`, `file:`, `gopher:`, and any non-HTTP scheme, and require TLS 1.3 where possible. Node.js supports TLS 1.3 and exposes the TLS 1.3 cipher suite names, with the three primary suites being `TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384`, `TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256`, and `TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256`. For production, use `minVersion: 'TLSv1.3'` for outbound clients that must be strictly modern, or `minVersion: 'TLSv1.2'` only when legacy interoperability is explicitly required and separately risk-accepted.

Strictly set certificate validation on, keep SNI bound to the original hostname, and never disable verification to “make it work”. The invariant is: **no TLS connection may proceed with certificate verification disabled, and no plaintext downgrade is allowed**.

```ts
import https from 'node:https';

export const outboundTlsOptions: https.RequestOptions = {
  minVersion: 'TLSv1.3',
  ciphers: [
    'TLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384',
    'TLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256',
    'TLS_AES_128_GCM_SHA256',
  ].join(':'),
  honorCipherOrder: true,
  rejectUnauthorized: true,
};
```

## 4. GNW_EGRESS_ALLOW_LIST semantics

Parse `GNW_EGRESS_ALLOW_LIST` as a structured policy, not as loose substring logic. The safest model is: exact host match, optional single-label or multi-label wildcard subdomain match, and explicit port matching; scheme should be fixed to HTTPS unless a particular exception is explicitly modeled. For example, `api.example.com:443` means only that exact host on port 443, and `*.example.com:443` means subdomains only, not the apex domain, unless apex is explicitly included.

A robust parser should canonicalize hostnames to lowercase, strip trailing dots, reject userinfo, reject empty labels, and compare ports numerically with the default port inferred from scheme when omitted. The invariant is: **an allowlist entry must match host pattern and effective port exactly, with no substring or suffix tricks**.

```ts
type AllowRule =
  | { kind: 'exact'; host: string; port?: number }
  | { kind: 'wildcard'; suffix: string; port?: number };

function parseAllowList(raw: string): AllowRule[] {
  return raw.split(',').map(s => s.trim()).filter(Boolean).map(item => {
    const [hostPort, portStr] = item.split(':');
    const port = portStr ? Number(portStr) : undefined;
    const host = hostPort.toLowerCase().replace(/\.$/, '');
    if (host.startsWith('*.')) return { kind: 'wildcard', suffix: host.slice(2), port };
    return { kind: 'exact', host, port };
  });
}

function hostMatches(rule: AllowRule, host: string, port: number): boolean {
  host = host.toLowerCase().replace(/\.$/, '');
  if (rule.port && rule.port !== port) return false;
  return rule.kind === 'exact'
    ? host === rule.host
    : host.endsWith('.' + rule.suffix) && host !== rule.suffix;
}
```

## 5. Redirect security

Do not allow automatic redirect following for untrusted outbound fetches unless you can re-run the full validation pipeline on every hop. The safest production default is to disable redirects; if redirects are needed, cap them at 3 hops, reject any scheme downgrade from HTTPS to HTTP, and re-validate the new URL, host, port, and resolved IP on each hop. Preserve the original security boundary by preventing a trusted host from bouncing the client to an internal or less secure target.

The invariant is: **each redirect target must be treated as a new request, with fresh URL parsing, allowlist matching, DNS resolution, address classification, and TLS policy enforcement**. If any hop fails validation, abort the entire request.

```ts
function assertRedirectAllowed(prevUrl: URL, nextUrl: URL): void {
  if (prevUrl.protocol === 'https:' && nextUrl.protocol !== 'https:') {
    throw new Error('SSRF_BLOCKED_PROTOCOL_DOWNGRADE');
  }
  if (nextUrl.protocol !== 'https:') throw new Error('SSRF_BLOCKED_NON_HTTPS_REDIRECT');
}
```

## 6. Timeouts and payload limits

Set a hard connect timeout of 5 seconds and a read timeout of 30 seconds, and enforce an absolute response payload ceiling of 10 MB. This protects against hanging sockets, slowloris-style reads, and memory exhaustion from unexpectedly large bodies. In Node.js, these limits should be enforced both at the socket level and at the stream consumption level, because a socket timeout alone does not cap body size.

The invariant is: **a request that exceeds either the time budget or the size budget must be aborted and logged as a security-relevant failure**. That includes chunked responses, compressed responses, and redirect chains that cumulatively exceed policy.

```ts
const MAX_BYTES = 10 * 1024 * 1024;

function enforceSizeLimit(stream: NodeJS.ReadableStream): Promise<Buffer> {
  return new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
    const chunks: Buffer[] = [];
    let total = 0;
    stream.on('data', (chunk: Buffer) => {
      total += chunk.length;
      if (total > MAX_BYTES) {
        reject(new Error('SSRF_BLOCKED_RESPONSE_TOO_LARGE'));
        stream.destroy();
        return;
      }
      chunks.push(chunk);
    });
    stream.on('end', () => resolve(Buffer.concat(chunks)));
    stream.on('error', reject);
  });
}
```

## 7. Proxy and namespace isolation

Egress should pass only through a controlled outbound proxy, with container or pod network policies denying direct Internet access except to that proxy. In Kubernetes, combine a restricted egress NetworkPolicy, a sidecar or node-local proxy, and security context hardening so the application container cannot bypass proxy controls by raw socket access. For stronger isolation, place the worker in a dedicated network namespace or sandbox such as gVisor/Firecracker, and ensure DNS resolution is also controlled because DNS itself is part of the attack surface.

Production invariants should require that the process can reach only approved proxy endpoints, and that proxy configuration is immutable at runtime. Never trust environment variables like `HTTP_PROXY` or `NO_PROXY` without explicit allowlisting, because they are common bypass vectors in server-side HTTP clients.

## 8. Immutable audit logging

Every outbound request should produce an append-only audit event containing timestamp, principal, canonical URL, resolved IP, rule decision, request hash, redirect count, response status, and byte count. To make logs tamper-evident, store them in an append-only table, stream them to immutable storage, or chain each record with a hash of the previous record. The request hash should be computed over the normalized request metadata, not the raw untrusted user input, so that the same logical request yields the same evidence record.

The invariant is: **every allowed or blocked egress attempt must be observable, and every audit record must be cryptographically linked or stored in WORM/immutable infrastructure**. That gives incident responders a trustworthy trail when a bypass attempt is detected.

```sql
CREATE TABLE egress_audit_log (
  id BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
  created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT now(),
  actor_id TEXT NOT NULL,
  canonical_url TEXT NOT NULL,
  resolved_ip TEXT,
  decision TEXT NOT NULL,
  request_hash BYTEA NOT NULL,
  response_status INT,
  response_bytes BIGINT,
  redirect_count INT NOT NULL DEFAULT 0,
  prev_record_hash BYTEA,
  record_hash BYTEA NOT NULL
);
```

```ts
import crypto from 'node:crypto';

function hashRequest(material: string): Buffer {
  return crypto.createHash('sha256').update(material).digest();
}
```

## 9. Circuit breaker

Trip the system into offline mode after 3 consecutive network egress violations, where “violation” means any policy rejection caused by non-HTTPS, private/special IP, allowlist mismatch, redirect downgrade, or other security control breach. The breaker should be per-tenant or per-principal when possible, so one abusive caller does not globally disable all egress for the platform. Once tripped, only local verification flows and previously approved cached artifacts should remain available until the breaker is manually or automatically cleared under controlled conditions.

The invariant is: **security control failures must be treated as signal, not noise, and repeated violations must force a safe degraded state**. In production, persist breaker state externally so process restarts do not silently clear it.

```ts
class CircuitBreaker {
  private failures = 0;
  private tripped = false;

  recordViolation(): void {
    this.failures += 1;
    if (this.failures >= 3) this.tripped = true;
  }

  canEgress(): boolean {
    return !this.tripped;
  }

  reset(): void {
    this.failures = 0;
    this.tripped = false;
  }
}
```

## 10. Air-gapped fallback

Air-gapped fallback mode should avoid external network dependency entirely and operate only on local verification assets, cached trust bundles, and locally replicated metadata. This means certificate chain validation, policy decisions, and any necessary artifact checks are performed from preloaded data, not live Internet lookups. In practice, that often means shipping the necessary CRLs, OCSP staples where applicable, pinned trust anchors, or signed policy bundles into the environment before deployment.

The invariant is: **when offline mode is active, the system must not attempt DNS resolution, external HTTP, or opportunistic network refreshes**. Any operation that cannot be satisfied locally should fail closed rather than silently weakening security.

```ts
export async function verifyInOfflineMode(input: unknown): Promise<boolean> {
  // local policy / signature / cache verification only
  return Boolean(input);
}
```

