## 10. Fail-closed recovery on migration verification failure

**Specification.** A failed migration transaction must roll back automatically; post-migration verification failure must stop deployment and route traffic away from the new application version. Do not assume a schema downgrade safely reverses data changes. Prefer forward-compatible expand/contract migrations, backups/PITR, and an explicit recovery plan.

```ts
export async function deployMigration(
  client: PoolClient,
  migrationId: string,
  migration: string[],
  verify: (client: PoolClient) => Promise<void>,
): Promise<void> {
  await runTransactionalMigration(client, migrationId, migration);

  try {
    await verify(client);
  } catch (verificationError) {
    // Fail closed: deployment controller must not promote this release.
    // Do not delete the migration marker or blindly execute down-migrations.
    throw new Error(
      `Migration ${migrationId} verification failed; deployment blocked`,
      { cause: verificationError },
    );
  }
}
```

Example verification checks can assert expected columns, constraints, policies, grants, and row counts. Run them under both the migration role (schema shape) and runtime role (effective access behavior). Have the deployment orchestrator keep the old application version serving traffic if verification fails, and alert operators.

**Important limitation:** a post-commit verification failure cannot be rolled back by a database transaction that has already committed. “Automated rollback” should therefore mean automatic transaction rollback before commit when migration execution fails, and automatic release/promotion rollback or traffic hold after verification failure—not an unsafe blind schema downgrade. Use tested backups and point-in-time recovery for unrecoverable data damage, consistent with organizational backup/recovery controls such as NIST SP 800-53 CP-9/CP-10.

## Standards and closure criteria

Use TLS certificate-chain and service-identity verification consistent with PostgreSQL’s `verify-full`, RFC 9525, RFC 8446, and NIST SP 800-52 Rev. 2.  Treat authorization, audit protection, and recovery as layered controls: least privilege, forced RLS, append-only audit protections, migration serialization, and tested recovery procedures.

Closure is achieved only when automated CI proves: TLS verification rejects invalid CA/hostname; all tenant tables force RLS; runtime credentials have no bypass or DDL authority; tenant-isolation negative tests pass; audit mutations are denied; SQL parameters are used consistently; migration failures roll back or block promotion; and the active health probe fails when read/write capability is unavailable.

